        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "1cb1d2998e3e2350c85513470f3ae1d5f33421ed",
            "parents": [
                "c76f964a8f793e295a149064df0fbc263ef97e93"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/81/78881/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Patrick Williams",
                "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                "username": "williamspatrick"
            },
            "createdOn": 1742322535,
            "author": {
                "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                "username": "ifel"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 23,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This is the bug that needs fixed.  our sort code shouldn't assume ID exists.  Do you know the line where it actually crashes (throws an exception) on this?\n\nI'm looking through odataObjectCmp, and it looks correct to me."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 23,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "Unfortunately no. The only thing I have is the `journalctl -xe -u bmcweb` output I put to the commit message."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 23,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "I did not have time to repro this, i.e use debug symbols/collect and analyze a core dump. However, one notice was, it did not fail immediately, i.e. if everything works the client receives result almost instantly, but if it's going to fail, it takes a few seconds after the server received request. So, it seems it reads memory, until it hits an address out of process' address space. That's my speculation."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 23,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "Sorry, why are you looking through odataObjectCmp? The [code](https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/master/redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp#L1514-L1517) does not use it, it defines lambda that explicitly compares Id fields. I briefly checked odataObjectCmp, we probably should change the comparison in the lambda, so, something like this:\n```\nstd::ranges::sort(entriesArray, [](const nlohmann::json& left,\n                                   const nlohmann::json& right) {\n        return odataObjectCmp(\"Id\", left, right);\n    });\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 23,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "Replaced \"return (left[\"Id\"] <= right[\"Id\"]);\" with \"return redfish::json_util::objectKeyCmp(\"Id\", left, right);\". This will prevent it from crashing even if fillEventLogLogEntryFromPropertyMap succeeds, but does not populate Id."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 23,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Sorry, trying to parse this quickly, but it sounds like you fixed the issue in the way I would've expected (using the appropriate utility functions).\n'\nThank you."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Tested, works for me \ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 1514,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Need to return internal error to the user here I would expect?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 1514,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "The error is being exposed to the client.\nWhen fillEventLogLogEntryFromPropertyMap returns false, it also calls \"messages::internalError(asyncResp->res);\". And as a result, the return will contain the error. It will not fail the whole request though. Take a look at [3] in the commit message, you will see that the errors are there, along with processed entries."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 1514,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "so then should this be \n\nif (!success){\n   return;\n}\n\nTo avoid duplicating internal error messages?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 1514,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "The error messages gets added by the `messages::internalError(asyncResp->res);` call, so by this time, it's already there. We can start doing interesting stuff - keeping the state (whether there was at least one error) here, pass it to the fillEventLogLogEntryFromPropertyMap and it will invoke messages::internalError only in case if no errors have registered yet. I don't think we should do that. Now client sees how many invalid entries there are, and can work on fixing them."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 1514,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> The error messages gets added by the `messages::internalError(asyncResp->res);` call, so by this time, it's already there. We can start doing interesting stuff - keeping the state (whether there was at least one error) here, pass it to the fillEventLogLogEntryFromPropertyMap and it will invoke messages::internalError only in case if no errors have registered yet. I don't think we should do that. Now client sees how many invalid entries there are, and can work on fixing them.\n\nWe shouldn't be posting N 500 errors to the user, given they're not actionable.  The code I suggested was to just stop execution where it was, and return a 500 error."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 1514,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Igor Kanyuka",
                        "email": "ifelmail@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ifel"
                    },
                    "message": "I wanted to provide a user with additional info, but you're right, it's enough to return 500, and then user will be able to find details in logs."
                }
            ],
